# Caddy configuration for cloudtak-plugin-print.
#
# Two steps. Neither one mentions your hostname, because every deployment names
# CloudTAK differently -- cloudtak.example.org, map.example.org, tak.example.org.
#
#
# STEP 1 -- paste this snippet at the TOP LEVEL of your Caddyfile, outside every
# site block, next to your other snippet definitions such as (rsa_cert).

(cloudtak_print) {
        # Both, not just /print-api/*. A path matcher of /print-api/* does NOT
        # match the bare /print-api, which is the info route the print panel calls
        # first -- it would fall through to CloudTAK's catch-all and come back as a
        # 200 of index.html. curl /print-api/health would pass the whole time.
        @cloudtak_print path /print-api /print-api/*

        handle @cloudtak_print {
                reverse_proxy cloudtak-print:5010
        }
}

# STEP 2 -- add one line inside the site block that already serves CloudTAK.
#
# Identify that block by its contents rather than its name: it is the one
# containing `reverse_proxy cloudtak-api:5000`.
#
#     yourhost.example.org {
#             import rsa_cert
#
#             import cloudtak_print          <-- add this
#
#             handle {
#                     reverse_proxy cloudtak-api:5000
#             }
#     }
#
# Caddy sorts handle blocks by path specificity, so the catch-all is evaluated
# last wherever you put the import; `caddy adapt` produces an identical route
# order either way (verified on Caddy 2.6.2). Putting it above the catch-all is
# still the clearer read.
#
#
# Then:
#
#     docker compose exec caddy caddy validate --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#     docker compose exec caddy caddy reload   --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
#
# `reload` refuses a bad config and leaves the running one untouched.
#
# Check BOTH paths afterwards. The health route passing proves only that
# /print-api/<something> routes, which is not what the panel calls first:
#
#     curl -sS https://YOURHOST/print-api/health
#     curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code} %{content_type}\n' https://YOURHOST/print-api
#
# The second must report application/json. text/html means CloudTAK's SPA is
# still answering and the snippet is not in effect.
#
#
# WHY A SAME-ORIGIN PATH rather than a print.* subdomain:
#
#   - the CloudTAK site block has no forward_auth, so XHR never meets Authentik
#     and never gets a 302 into a login page
#   - CSP connect-src 'self' already covers it (see api/nginx.conf.js)
#   - no new certificate, no new DNS record
#
# /print-api and not /print because CloudTAK's SPA is the catch-all handle {},
# so a Vue route at /print would be intercepted here on a hard reload.
#
# Caddy reaches cloudtak-print by container name, so Caddy and the print service
# must share a Docker network. In the tak-stack compose file they already do.
